<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Temple of Edfu</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">237–<a href="/years/57-bce">57 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a> · Also known as Apollonopolis Magna</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edfu">Edfu</a> is an Egyptian temple on the west bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> at Edfu in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-egypt">Upper Egypt</a>, built under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemaic-kingdom">Ptolemaic Kingdom</a> between 237 and 57 BCE and dedicated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horus">Horus</a>. Begun under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-iii-euergetes">Ptolemy III Euergetes</a> and completed under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-xii-auletes">Ptolemy XII Auletes</a> on the site of an earlier temple of Horus, it is one of the best preserved shrines in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and the largest temple dedicated to Horus and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hathor">Hathor</a> of Dendera. Its inscribed walls record its building, its creation myth and the conflict between Horus and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seth">Seth</a>, and each year Hathor's journey from Dendera to Edfu was marked by a great festival. Buried up to 12 metres deep in sand and silt, it was cleared from 1860 by Auguste Mariette.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/temple-of-edfu.jpg" alt="Temple of Edfu" width="330" height="247" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Patrick.reb · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Temple_Edfou_Egypte.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
